it's usually with smaller functions like
```c
s32 add(s32 a, s32 b) {
    return a + b;
}

void do_something() {
    s32 c = add(1, 2);
    // do other stuff with c
}
//gets turned into
void do_something() {
    s32 c = 1 + 2;
    // do other stuff with c
} 
```

when the objects are compiled, all of the functions are kept and are capable of being linked against. so if I have a header/source pair that looks like this:
```cpp
// Header

extern void do_something( int x );
extern void do_something_else( int y );

void f( int x );
void g( int x, int y );

// Source

void f( int x )
{
    do_something( x );
}

void g( int x, int y )
{
    f( x );
    do_something_else( y );
}
```
the object file has definitions for both `f` and `g`. however, because of `-ipa file`, `g` itself no longer calls `f`, but is instead optimized to just rewrite `f` inside of `g`

if the function is defined in the header itself:
```diff
void g( int x, int y );
+ inline void h( int x )
+ {
+   g( x, 0 );
+ }
```
this has an annoying property where it never shows up as unused because it's not defined in a source file. instead, if the program never uses it, it's completely impossible to know if h exists or not
